/-
# HyperbolicIsometryInversion.lean (C2) — inversion in a boundary sphere is a
# `dH`-isometry, DERIVED.

The inversion `x ↦ c + (R/‖x-c‖)²·(x-c)` in a sphere centred at a boundary point
`c` (`c_z = 0`) is the generator that makes the isometry group of `H³` act
transitively — the one NOT reachable from the boundary similarity group
(reflections + dilations + horizontal translations).  Here it is proved to
preserve the derived hyperbolic distance `dH`, with no cited geometry.

## The mechanism (the "sweat")

The Fréchet derivative of the inversion at `p` is CONFORMAL:
`Dφ(p) = (R/‖p-c‖)² · (orthogonal reflection)` — a positive scalar `lam(p) =
(R/‖p-c‖)²` times a linear isometry (Mathlib's `hasFDerivAt_inversion`).  So it
scales every tangent norm by `lam(p)`.  And the height scales by the SAME factor:
`(φ p)_z = (R/‖p-c‖)² · p_z` (because `c_z = 0`).  The line element `‖v‖/z` is
therefore untouched:

    ‖Dφ(p)·v‖ / (φ p)_z  =  lam(p)·‖v‖ / (lam(p)·p_z)  =  ‖v‖ / p_z.

That exact-cancellation is what `HyperbolicMetric.dH_image_eq` consumes, and the
inversion is involutive (`inversion_involutive`), so it is a full `dH`-isometry.

Archimedean (ℝ): `‖·‖`, `dist`, ordering — §7c.

  -- Ref: [milnor1982] §1 (isometries generated by boundary inversions)
  -- Ref: [thurston1978] Ch. 2 §2.4 (isometries of the upper half-space model)
  -- Ref: [ratcliffe] §4.6 (inversions are hyperbolic isometries)
  -- Ref: [leitner2026] beans qou-473l (T2 C2), qou-idvt (T2)
-/

import QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicMetric
import Mathlib.Geometry.Euclidean.Inversion.Calculus

namespace QOU.Archimedean

open QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicMetric EuclideanGeometry
open scoped ENNReal

/-- **The conformal height-scaling data of a boundary inversion.**  On `H³` (where
`p ≠ c`), the inversion's Fréchet derivative is `(R/‖p-c‖)²·(orthogonal
reflection)` — a positive scalar times a linear isometry — so it scales every
tangent norm by `lam(p) = (R/‖p-c‖)²`, and (because `c_z = 0`) it scales the
height by the SAME factor. -/
noncomputable def boundaryInversionIsom {c : E} (hc : c 2 = 0) {R : ℝ} (hR : 0 < R) :
    IsDHIsometry (inversion c R : E → E) where
  Dφ := fun p => (R / dist p c) ^ 2 • ((ℝ ∙ (p - c))ᗮ.reflection : E →L[ℝ] E)
  lam := fun p => (R / dist p c) ^ 2
  hderiv := by
    -- the inversion's Fréchet derivative on `H³` (where `p ≠ c`)
    intro p hp
    have hp2 : 0 < p 2 := hp
    have hpc : p ≠ c := by
      intro h; rw [h, hc] at hp2; exact lt_irrefl 0 hp2
    exact hasFDerivAt_inversion hpc
  hlam := by
    -- the conformal factor is strictly positive
    intro p hp
    have hp2 : 0 < p 2 := hp
    have hpc : p ≠ c := by
      intro h; rw [h, hc] at hp2; exact lt_irrefl 0 hp2
    have hd : 0 < dist p c := dist_pos.mpr hpc
    positivity
  hconf := by
    -- `Dφ(p)` scales every norm by the conformal factor
    intro p hp v
    have hp2 : 0 < p 2 := hp
    have hpc : p ≠ c := by
      intro h; rw [h, hc] at hp2; exact lt_irrefl 0 hp2
    have hd : 0 < dist p c := dist_pos.mpr hpc
    have hrefl : ‖((ℝ ∙ (p - c))ᗮ.reflection : E →L[ℝ] E) v‖ = ‖v‖ := by
      have : ((ℝ ∙ (p - c))ᗮ.reflection : E →L[ℝ] E) v
          = (ℝ ∙ (p - c))ᗮ.reflection v := rfl
      rw [this]; exact LinearIsometryEquiv.norm_map _ _
    rw [ContinuousLinearMap.smul_apply, norm_smul, Real.norm_eq_abs,
      abs_of_nonneg (by positivity), hrefl]
  hheight := by
    -- the height scales by the SAME conformal factor (because `c_z = 0`)
    intro p hp
    have hexp : inversion c R p = (R / dist p c) ^ 2 • (p - c) + c := by
      rw [show inversion c R p = (R / dist p c) ^ 2 • (p -ᵥ c) +ᵥ c from rfl,
        vsub_eq_sub, vadd_eq_add]
    show (inversion c R p) 2 = (R / dist p c) ^ 2 * (p 2)
    rw [hexp, PiLp.add_apply, PiLp.smul_apply, PiLp.sub_apply, smul_eq_mul, hc]
    ring

/-- **C2 — inversion in a boundary sphere preserves the hyperbolic distance.**
For a centre `c` on the boundary `∂H³` (`c_z = 0`) and radius `R > 0`, the
inversion `EuclideanGeometry.inversion c R` is a `dH`-isometry — DERIVED from the
conformal-height-scaling engine (`IsDHIsometry.dH_eq`), not adopted; the inversion
is involutive. -/
theorem dH_boundaryInversion {c : E} (hc : c 2 = 0) {R : ℝ} (hR : 0 < R) (x y : E) :
    dH (inversion c R x) (inversion c R y) = dH x y :=
  (boundaryInversionIsom hc hR).dH_eq (inversion_involutive c (ne_of_gt hR)) x y

end QOU.Archimedean
